title: 'pallet-revive: extend the stipend reentrancy guard to value transfers'
doc:
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    A contract that receives value through Solidity's `transfer()` or `send()` can no longer call back into the sender. This already held for zero-value transfers and now also applies when value moves. What the receiver sees depends on the VM it runs on:

    - EVM: The reentrant call returns 0 to the receiver; before #12673 it ran out of gas, which reverted the transfer.
    - PolkaVM: The reentrant call aborts the receiver, so the transfer reverts, as before.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `integrity_test` checks that a block cannot write more contract storage than a validator has memory for. It scaled a write's weight by the memory that write uses before dividing the block weight by it, so the storage term came out near zero and the check could not fail on it. It now computes how many writes fit the block weight before multiplying by the memory each one uses.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: minor

